---
// Release 1 results. Renders the public summary committed in the fedcourtsai
// repo, read at the results/ot2026-longconf tag by scripts/fetch-results.mjs.
// Until that tag exists the page shows its pending state, is left out of the
// nav, and asks not to be indexed. Nothing here is computed or edited: the
// summary is shown as committed, and on any disagreement the repo wins.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/ModelMirrorAI/fedcourtsai";
const metaFile = Object.values(import.meta.glob("../data/results/meta.json", { eager: true, import: "default" }))[0];
const summary = Object.values(import.meta.glob("../data/results/summary.md", { eager: true }))[0];
const meta = metaFile ?? { state: "pending" };
const published = meta.state === "published" && summary;
const Content = summary?.Content;
const short = meta.sha ? meta.sha.slice(0, 7) : null;
---
<Base
  title="Results"
  description={published
    ? "The first scored results: AI forecasts of the Supreme Court’s first conference of the October 2026 Term, each committed before the Court acted."
    : "Results publish after the Court acts."}
  noindex={!published}
  wide={Boolean(published)}
>
  <h1>Results</h1>

  {published ? (
    <>
      <p>
        <span class="stamp">
          Release 1 · tag <a href={meta.release_url}>{meta.tag}</a>{short && <> · commit <a href={`${REPO}/commit/${meta.sha}`}>{short}</a></>}
        </span>
      </p>
      <article class="results-body">
        <Content />
      </article>
      <p class="results-source"><small>
        This page is the <a href={meta.summary_url}>public summary</a> committed at the tag above, shown without changes.{" "}
        Every figure in it is copied from the <a href={meta.audit_url}>audit write-up</a> or the release’s dataset export,{" "}
        and if this page and either one disagree, the source is right and this page is wrong.
      </small></p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <>
      <p class="lead">Results publish after the Court acts.</p>

      <p>
        The first scored release covers the Supreme Court’s first conference of the October 2026 Term, on September 28, 2026.{" "}
        Before it, three AI models each forecast whether the Court would take up a set of pending petitions, and every forecast{" "}
        was merged into the public <a href="/ledger">ledger</a> before the Court met.
      </p>

      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>The conference.</strong> The Justices meet on September 28, 2026. Forecasts for it are already on the ledger.</li>
        <li><strong>The order list.</strong> The Court announces which petitions it granted and denied, expected on or around October 5.</li>
        <li><strong>Grading and audit.</strong> Each forecast is graded against the Court’s action and the figures are checked.</li>
        <li><strong>Publication.</strong> The write-up is tagged in the repository, archived on Zenodo, and shown here.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Already fixed</h2>
      <p>
        What this page will show, and how, was committed before any outcome existed. The{" "}
        <a href={`${REPO}/commits/main/docs/release-ot2026-public-summary.md`}>public summary template</a> sets out every table
        and the rules for filling it in: no placeholder is replaced by an estimate, no single score is pooled across models or
        groups of petitions, and every number carries its count. The instructions and settings the models ran under are fixed by the{" "}
        <a href={`${REPO}/releases/tag/prereg%2Fproc-v8`}><code>prereg/proc-v8</code></a> tag, archived as{" "}
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22966597">doi:10.5281/zenodo.22966597</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Until then, the <a href="/ledger">ledger</a> lists every forecast as it was committed, and{" "}
        <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a> explains how to check that a forecast came before the outcome.
      </p>
    </>
  )}
</Base>

<style>
  .lead { font-size: 1.2rem; }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3em; }
  .steps li { margin: 0.5em 0; }

  /* The summary's own sections: its first heading is the release headline. */
  .results-body { max-width: var(--wide); }
  .results-body :global(> h2:first-child) { font-size: 1.45rem; margin-top: 0.5rem; }
  .results-body :global(p), .results-body :global(ul) { max-width: var(--measure); }
  /* Tables scroll inside themselves on narrow screens; the page never does. */
  .results-body :global(table) { display: block; overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; margin-bottom: 1.4em; }
  .results-body :global(th) { white-space: nowrap; }
  .results-body :global(td:first-child) { min-width: 12ch; }
  .results-source { max-width: var(--measure); margin-top: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 1rem; }
</style>
